## This turn's refactoring: `TypeRenderUtils` chain-walk deduplication (`core` module)

`getSuperClass(Class)` and `getClassloader(Class)` implemented the identical algorithm — walk a linked chain from a seed node, collecting each node's string form into an array — differing only in three points: the seed (`getSuperclass()` vs `getClassLoader()`), the step (`getSuperclass()` vs `getParent()`), and the string conversion (`StringUtils.classname` vs `toString`).

I extracted a small generic helper and reduced both public methods to one line each:

```java
public static String[] getSuperClass(Class clazz) {
    return walkChain(clazz.getSuperclass(), Class::getSuperclass, StringUtils::classname);
}

public static String[] getClassloader(Class clazz) {
    return walkChain(clazz.getClassLoader(), ClassLoader::getParent, ClassLoader::toString);
}

private static <T> String[] walkChain(T head, UnaryOperator<T> next, Function<T, String> toString) {
    List<String> list = new ArrayList<String>();
    T node = head;
    while (node != null) {
        list.add(toString.apply(node));
        node = next.apply(node);
    }
    return list.toArray(new String[0]);
}
```

The original `if (head != null) { add(head); while(true){ step; if null break; add; } }` shape is exactly equivalent to the helper's `while (node != null) { add; step; }`, so the output is identical. Method references keep it readable, and lambdas/method-refs are already used elsewhere in the codebase (e.g. `ProcessUtils`), so this fits the existing style rather than introducing a new one.

**Added tests:** `getSuperClass`/`getClassloader` were previously untested. I added two characterization tests using self-contained nested classes (`Parent`/`Child`) for determinism: superclass chain up to `Object` (and empty for `Object` itself), and the classloader chain (empty for a bootstrap-loaded class, first element matching the class's own loader). I ran them against the **unmodified** code first to establish a baseline, then re-ran after the refactor — both green.

